WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:05.580
- [Mark] All right, Tony, Tony Borges?

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:06.670
- Tony Borges, yes.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:07.840
- [Mark] Tony, you have a

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.130
very interesting project you're doing,

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:11.436
a housing project that involves--

00:00:11.436 --> 00:00:14.270
- Yes, we're doing housing for

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.290
pre-schools,

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:18.650
commercial

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:20.920
and for residential.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:21.970
And

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:23.130
10 years ago,

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.750
I came up with this system

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.640
to build for the homeless

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.210
and laid it all out 10 years ago,

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:32.043
had

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.270
marketed a little bit

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.720
when we were doing the schools

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:38.553
and

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:40.303
it just,

00:00:40.303 --> 00:00:42.560
it never came across.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.620
And then I rethought what I was doing,

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.950
and I came up with,

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:51.820
I thought a better solution
for what we're doing.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.000
And the solution is to hire

00:00:55.643 --> 00:00:57.850
5-10% of the homeless

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.070
to build the structures.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:04.343
And we've been building
these structures since 1980.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.330
We've done it where we've went to islands

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:11.383
and took non-skilled labor to build them.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:15.330
So we've been doing it forever.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.000
But what I did from the ones that

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.580
we did nine years ago,

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.840
they were smaller houses.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.570
Now I came out with houses

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:26.930
that would be for the women and kids.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.370
One for the women and women,

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.333
the other will be for men and men.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:33.730
We can make them two story.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:35.780
They can go on a,

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:36.940
from a one-acre

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:39.693
to a five to ten acre site of land.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.740
They can be also,

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:45.170
the men could be two-story

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.270
if we get a tight site.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:48.670
We're right now,

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:52.670
we're putting the funds together
to start from San Pedro,

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.770
to Lancaster to build 12 facilities.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.470
- [Mark] So, these are
homeless communities?

00:01:57.470 --> 00:01:58.670
that were homeless
- Homeless community.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:01.910
Now, to give you an idea
of the homeless community,

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:05.210
if you're familiar with
like a mobile home park,

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:06.820
they're like a mobile home park,

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:08.760
a mini mobile home park,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.520
except their really buildings,

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:12.560
the same buildings we build the schools

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:13.600
the same quality.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.240
- [Mark] So you have a building process?

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:16.073
- Yeah, we have.

00:02:16.073 --> 00:02:16.906
- [Mark] The process is very unique.

00:02:16.906 --> 00:02:19.430
- Yeah, we've been doing this since 1980.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:20.399
- [Mark] And tell me about

00:02:20.399 --> 00:02:21.745
the process of building it.

00:02:21.745 --> 00:02:24.250
- We've been building schools.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.250
There's a company called DSA

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.340
or state architects

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.000
called DSA.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.030
All this product is built under DSA

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:35.820
requirements like.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.681
So it's all,

00:02:37.681 --> 00:02:38.514
all ADA

00:02:38.514 --> 00:02:39.970
all handicap,

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:42.500
could be anything that we wanna build

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.150
out of this product.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:45.350
So that's what we're doing.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:46.960
- [Mark] And there's,

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:48.890
the structures are built
fairly quickly right?

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:49.723
- Yeah.

00:02:49.723 --> 00:02:51.670
Well, they're made in a factory

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:53.550
and they take them out to the job site.

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:54.880
That's where we trained the people

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.340
to actually put them back together

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:58.700
and then also

00:02:59.536 --> 00:03:02.669
erect them and finish
them on the job site.

00:03:02.669 --> 00:03:03.502
- [Mark] How many days,

00:03:03.502 --> 00:03:05.204
how long does it take to build?

00:03:05.204 --> 00:03:08.080
- Once the building gets on the job site,

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:09.470
five days.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:10.830
- [Mark] Five days to build a

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:12.890
- An average of--
how many square foot house?

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:16.790
- They are 540 feet up to 600 feet.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:18.410
- [Mark] So small structures, but...

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:19.610
- Well, yeah, but I mean,

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:20.670
they're very high quality.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.680
The nine-foot high ceilings,

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.580
we didn't cut any corners.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:27.820
But these homes are also fire resistant.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:29.560
There's no wood in them,

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:31.480
but they're just the same buildings

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:32.980
that we've built schools out of.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:34.200
- [Mark] And you use select

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:37.840
workers from the homeless community?

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:38.673
You would train them?
- Yes!

00:03:38.673 --> 00:03:39.910
Yeah, train them, yeah.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:41.400
At one time,

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.980
we had the factory about

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:46.510
25, 30 years ago

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:47.343
in,

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:49.910
in Torrance.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:53.120
And I moved the factory to Compton

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.360
and I couldn't find any workers,

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:56.960
so I'd get the workers--

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:58.770
- [Mark] Because no one
wants to go to Compton.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:03:59.610
- Yeah, yeah.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.607
- There was black people there and I go,

00:04:01.607 --> 00:04:05.240
that's okay, I've been all over Africa.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:06.720
I was in Africa in

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:07.700
1959/

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.780
60

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:13.200
with the US government
building water projects

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.790
and sewer systems for Africa.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:18.380
So I've been around working with people,

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.550
but when I moved to Compton,

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:23.290
my workers wouldn't go to Compton.

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:25.750
So I would get them from the jail,

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:28.160
they would burst them in from the jail.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:29.980
And we were at one time building

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:32.960
four houses an hour in the factory.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:35.610
This was a little 52-meter house,

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:36.730
544

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:37.840
feet.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:39.850
And we'd take non-skilled labor

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.109
and build them in the factory.

00:04:42.109 --> 00:04:43.250
Okay.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:44.083
- [Mark] All right.

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:46.550
- That went when Reagan was in,

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:47.630
they were going for

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:49.150
the,

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:51.150
the Contra missile people

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:53.940
when they had those
homes were developed for.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:56.180
- [Mark] So do you have any support from

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:57.920
local governments or anything like that?

00:04:57.920 --> 00:04:59.620
- No, we're we,

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:03.300
I bypass the local government right now

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.160
because the two or three times

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:07.010
that I have talked to them,

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:08.180
or the

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:09.013
couple

00:05:09.013 --> 00:05:10.070
they got their money

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:11.320
all set aside

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:14.660
like the schools.

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:16.740
And that's why the buildings are costing

00:05:17.656 --> 00:05:19.980
$400,000 to $600,000

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:21.300
a unit.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.820
And these buildings that we're
building in the factory costs

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:27.000
only about 85,000.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.630
Time you put them on the land

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:30.560
and do the site work,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.520
you're probably 150,000.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:36.360
So it's about half the price,

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:37.600
half the quality.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:40.570
And these are also the solar-solar

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:41.673
solar powered.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:43.393
- [Mark] Oh, they're solar?

00:05:43.393 --> 00:05:44.226
- Yeah, they're solar.

00:05:44.226 --> 00:05:46.580
So everything we've been doing

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:49.540
is what we're doing for here.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:51.530
So that's the same quality.

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:53.850
We are also working on

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.640
building fire-resistant homes

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:58.703
and the fire-resistant homes

00:05:58.703 --> 00:06:00.253
that we're gonna build,

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:02.590
gonna go out in Paradise.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:03.940
And as you know, fire,

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:07.170
but it's a home that has no wood.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:09.960
We have a product that we developed

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:11.270
that is UL approved.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:13.840
Gives you a three-hour firewall.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:15.830
Now like up in Paradise,

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:17.560
where they had the big fires,

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.650
We also make buildings

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:22.110
that would be where people can go in there

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.610
and lock theirself in

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:26.193
for 72 hours,

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:29.530
and let the fire burn by

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:30.660
and go from there.

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:33.390
- [Mark] So the home is
actually a protection.

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:34.223
- Yeah.

00:06:34.223 --> 00:06:37.020
Now when we start doing this,

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:39.630
we're gonna open up three major factories

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:41.630
in LA County.

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:44.660
Okay, one factory will
be to build the kit.

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:46.300
We call them kit.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:47.980
The next one will be building

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.810
the equipment to build the panels.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.090
And then the next one will be

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:53.670
the thermal shield

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:57.570
which actually is a
product that we developed

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:58.920
that you put on a building

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:01.960
and it gives you a three-hour firewall.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:03.800
So that product but it also

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:06.370
not only gives you a three-hour firewall,

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:08.040
the other thing that it does

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:12.150
it actually drops the
temperature 35 40 degrees.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.130
If a house is in the coal area,

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:15.000
it keeps it warm.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.850
If it's in a hot area, it keeps it cool.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:20.230
But it's a product that
we used on the school.

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:24.160
It's DSA approved and it's UL approved.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:26.183
Okay, it's a product that we develop.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:28.800
So that's what we're up to.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.970
But then this building business,

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:33.280
I've been doing this product

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:34.113
since

00:07:34.113 --> 00:07:34.946
1980,

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:37.570
we had some good runs

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:41.000
with big companies like Morrison, Knutson

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:43.930
and companies like that, that
we did in the Philippines

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:46.030
and different other countries

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:47.480
that we did things for.

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:49.570
But we've been doing this forever.

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:52.250
Our background was concrete.

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.690
We know more about concrete than anybody.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:58.810
I started building
everything from Oroville dam,

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:01.370
to missile silos and in

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:04.050
Santa Maria

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:05.500
up there humpback.

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:08.550
So we've been in the concrete business.

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:10.180
We did Bart in the Bay area.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:13.160
We did a lot of work at the LA airport

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.420
and all the parking structures.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:17.500
And we did dams.

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:19.530
We did three dams.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:22.250
They were supposed to
build 23 dams in the state.

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:23.760
We did three of them,

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:25.860
and then they ran out of money.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:28.417
But we came down with
the federal river project

00:08:28.417 --> 00:08:31.180
Orville all the way to San Diego

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:34.150
doing different parts of the project.

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:36.140
So our background was concrete.

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:38.060
When I was in Africa,

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:40.990
I tried to build concrete home.

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:42.970
Thought, well I'm in
the concrete business,

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:45.170
I should be able to build concrete home.

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:47.500
It seems like it works.

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.150
You end up with a box,

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:51.440
but the costs and the labor

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:54.080
and the amount of equipment you need

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.670
makes it not feasible.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:59.340
Okay, so what we're doing now,

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:01.970
the three plants that
we're setting up here

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:03.250
in LA,

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:04.531
they will partake this

00:09:04.531 --> 00:09:06.430
and then we are gonna take it to

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:08.070
four sites

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:10.470
in LA or LA County.

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:12.750
And the site will have a school

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:15.120
have the thing to train the people

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:18.950
how to put them back together.

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:21.400
And then also the job site

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:23.007
we're gonna need anywhere from

00:09:23.007 --> 00:09:25.340
2-4,000 people

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:26.370
to,

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:27.900
to start this project

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:28.920
- [Mark] Having employment for a lot

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:30.800
of these people would change the lives.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:31.937
- Yeah, yeah.

00:09:31.937 --> 00:09:32.982
- [Mark] And then they would,

00:09:32.982 --> 00:09:33.815
this would be ongoing
employment for them, or--?

00:09:33.815 --> 00:09:35.300
- We're gonna, oh yeah.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:39.060
They'll be, because when
we get them trained,

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:42.023
we'll be doing work in Paradise.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:44.930
And what we're gonna
do is put what we call

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:47.050
them a satellite plant.

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:49.170
So we're gonna take the same thing,

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:50.057
put it up there.

00:09:50.057 --> 00:09:52.720
And the people that we're training here,

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:54.310
will go there

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:55.143
and,

00:09:55.143 --> 00:09:56.490
and work

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:57.748
up there in that job,

00:09:57.748 --> 00:10:00.100
the company that's putting in the money

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:03.470
wants us to have 10,000 people to work.

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:04.303
So,

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:06.140
as you know,

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:08.124
I'm getting calls from

00:10:08.124 --> 00:10:09.703
San Jose,

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:14.883
San Francisco, we're talking
to some people in Washington.

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:19.320
We know we got a deal in Texas.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:20.621
And in Florida.

00:10:20.621 --> 00:10:21.721
- [Mark] So this would

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:24.670
potentially be a new location

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:26.170
for the homeless
communities in these cities?

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:27.003
- Oh, yeah.

00:10:27.003 --> 00:10:27.836
Now, these are,

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:30.970
go back and look at the homeless,

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:32.600
and what is people don't,

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:35.210
they don't want anybody in their backyard.

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:38.410
But if you put them in a gated community,

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:40.570
a secured gated community,

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:43.780
and select the ones that
you're gonna put in there

00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:45.710
under qualification,

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:46.543
okay,

00:10:46.543 --> 00:10:47.480
we know that some of them

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.920
that need medical help
and different things,

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:52.060
but this is actually a home.

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:55.510
What we have to do is find
a way to bring some dignity

00:10:56.980 --> 00:10:59.480
back into the homeless market.

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:00.650
Because right now,

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:03.140
all they're doing is
just taking the money,

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:04.600
putting it in their own pocket,

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:06.150
the big boys

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:08.000
and they're not doing

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:09.680
what they should be doing.

